  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 203 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThursday...More beneficial rains are anticipated as a decent \npositively tilted shortwave trough aloft passes overhead near peak \nheating, and with beaucoup, plenty of low and mid level moisture and \ndecent upslope southeast winds advecting low level moisture \nnorthwestward, expect to see numerous to widespread showers with \nscattered thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening, focused once \nagain along the I-25 corridor and near the mountains where \norographics will coincide with the best 850/700 mb theta-e ridge \naxis. POPS along the spine of the Laramie Range and the Interstate \n25 corridor will range from 60 to 90 percent, and weak steering flow \naloft seen near 700 mb, around 10,000 feet Mean Sea Level, MSL, will \naid in some locally heavy rainfall and some training of storms.\n\nFriday...Will see an abrupt cutoff to the low and mid level moisture \nsupply as drier air at low and mid levels advects into our counties \nas the dominant ridge over Colorado and southern Wyoming reasserts \nitself. Thus dry and hotter.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday...Isolated to scattered afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms expected, with areal coverage \nincreasing each day due to increased mid level moisture. \nFortunately, more beneficial rains expected concentrated over and \nnear our Snowy, Sierra Madre and Laramie Ranges, with the best \nchances for convection on Monday and Tuesday. Typical weak northwest \nflow aloft on the northeast periphery of the ridge aloft will aid in \noccasional passing shortwave troughs aloft near peak heating \nresulting in increasing convective potential each day",
